directory commands
whoami : it itewlls which user you are using right now

echo : output some specific text that provider => echo "hello world!"

cat : show the content of a file => cat notepad.txt

cd : change directory => cd ./my-directory

ls : show list of files => ls , ls -a : it show also hidden files

pwd : it prints all way to current directory

find : search for file by their name => find -name pasword.txt

grep : search inside for text for example => grep "pasword123" pasword.txt

touch : create file => touch note.txt

cp : copy file => cp note.txt | cp note.txt ./newfolder

mv : move or rename file and directory => mv note.txt ../ |  mv note.text rename.txt

rm : remove file or directory

file : identitify file type => file note.txt

su : switch user => su Admin1

operators 

& : runs the command but does not wait for it to finish before you can do any thing else. the command runs in the background.

&& : run both command but wait for the first command ti finish first

> : used to redirect output. we can take the output of a command and send it to a file

>> : this redirector does the something but instead of overwriting. it will just add the output to the bottom of the file.

ls -l : rw-r-r it shows file permission => first part for root, second part for group, third part for other

chmod : it means change mode you can change permissions => chmode 640 normal.txt

chown : change owner of file  => chown  user note.txt

groups : display groups of user